        {
          "type": "TextualBody",
          "label": "verso",
          "value": "<p>I am mired in debt and hunger, a for[eigner, alone, who has no one] except you and God. Do not abandon me, my brother; [I swear] by the truth of these letters that Passover came and passed, and I was [only] able [to buy] a small measure of beets that were boiled [word unclear]. I didn\u2019t have a single piece of silver. Instead, I cut a nettle from the ruins and boiled it. The night of Purim, when the people were eating and drinking, I boiled and ate [word unclear] for dinner. We spent every evening in the house\u2014my daughter and I. Even though I\u2019ve told you what I\u2019ve told you, hearing is not the same as witnessing [my suffering]. You, my brother, help strangers and people from abroad, so how can [you not help] me, your sister? Now, my brother, help me with the matter of this debt in which I have been mired. Do not abandon me. Do not forsake me. Less than this, my brother, would be enough for you.</p>\n<p>May God grant you life and protect you. May I not be deprived, my brother, of your countenance, and may we be reunited.</p>\n<p>I have not advised you, my brother, concerning Tamma\u0304m. He is your son, who has no one besides you and God. May God grant you life, and may you not neglect this matter\u2014may God grant you life. I will not give you any advice, by God, my brother, may you be granted the best of peace. My son-in-law sends wishes of peace\u2014and my daughter. And peace to you.</p>\n<p>And I, \u2018Al\u016bsh the shammash [beadle], send you wishes for the best peace, and similarly all our colleagues wish you peace.</p>\n<p></p>",
          "format": "text/html"
        }
      ],
      "motivation": [
        "sc:supplementing",
        "translating"
      ],
      "schema:position": 1
    },
